# IPC & Main Process

All main↔renderer communication goes through named channels defined once in
`@shared/types`. Adding a feature that crosses the process boundary touches four
files in this order.

## Adding a channel end-to-end

1. `src/shared/types/index.ts` — add the channel to the `IpcChannels` `as const`
   object (`domain:action` string), plus any payload/return types.
2. `src/main/<feature>.ts` — register the handler inside an exported
   `register<Feature>Handlers()` function using `ipcMain.handle`.
3. `src/main/index.ts` — call `register<Feature>Handlers()` inside
   `app.whenReady()`.
4. `src/preload/index.ts` — add a method to `electronApi` that wraps
   `ipcRenderer.invoke`, and declare it on the global `ElectronApi` interface in
   `src/preload/api.d.ts`.

## Main-process handlers

- Treat every incoming argument as `unknown` and validate it with a type guard
  before use; reject invalid input by throwing an `Error` with a user-facing
  message (see `registerModelHandlers`, `isValidTheme`, `normalizeShortcuts`).
- Log meaningful events and every rejection with `logger` (`info`/`warn`/`error`).
- Persist state through `getStoreValue`/`setStoreValue` against the typed
  `StoreSchema` in `main/store` — don't touch `electron-store` directly elsewhere.
- Stream long-running output by sending events to `webContents` (guard with
  `isDestroyed()`), not by resolving the invoke; support cancellation with
  `AbortController` (see `main/llama`).

## Preload bridge

- The preload layer is the trust boundary: normalize/validate values coming back
  from `invoke` (cast `unknown`, coerce with helpers like `normalizeShortcuts`)
  so the renderer receives well-typed, safe data.
- Expose exactly one `electronApi` object via `contextBridge`; never leak
  `ipcRenderer` to the renderer.
- Subscription methods (`on...`) return an unsubscribe function that calls
  `removeListener`.
